 "genre_note": "133 notes over 20 Verge documents (46,369 words); 45,549 words assigned = 98.2% coverage, verified programmatically for zero duplicate (doc, block) assignments, zero unaccounted blocks, and no note drawing on more than 1,800 source words (largest: 1,285).\n\nBUILDING-BLOCK DISTRIBUTION. empirical_observation dominates (~76 notes) and concept is second (~16), exactly as expected for news. argument (14) and counter_argument (10) occur genuinely and were not manufactured: the seven FTX-trial columns are explicitly argumentative (the missing chief risk officer as the hole in the defense case; the money trail refuting the Effective Altruism claim; why the code record outlives deleted Signal messages), and adversarial-proceeding coverage supplies real rebuttals (each defense cross-examination is a counter_argument against a witness's direct testimony). Reviews likewise carry a claim/rebuttal spine (Meta's AI assistant \"isn't really smart\"; double tap's customization limits versus Apple's \"just works\" claim). model (13) covers mechanisms \u2014 the allow_negative liquidation exemption, Google's relevance/distance/prominence local ranking, the S9's neural engines cutting through wrist-sensor noise, Roblox's three AI clouds. procedure (10) occurs only in doc_0403, which is a genuine how-to reference (contact this number, bring the ad, within N days); nothing else in the cluster is step-shaped, and I did not force any. hypothesis occurs EXACTLY ONCE, in doc_0539: Baszucki's testable prediction that someone will build a dating app on Roblox for ID-verified 17+ users. No navigation notes were produced.\n\nSHARED ENTITY NOTES. Seven FTX docs are seven daily columns about one trial, so 12 notes draw blocks from two to five documents rather than being duplicated per-document: ftx_fraud_charges_and_sec_allegation (5 docs \u2014 the standing boilerplate), alameda_65_billion_line_of_credit_at_ftx (4), ftx_alameda_allow_negative_liquidation_exemption (3), alameda_absorbed_ftx_losses_mobilecoin (3), ftx_collapse_and_bankruptcy_november_2022 (3), plus 7 two-doc notes. This is what lets one note satisfy several pieces of evidence.\n\nSCOPE CONDITION FOUND IN \"CHROME\". The standing trial boilerplate looks like a repeated ad-block, but doc_0053's version states \"has been found guilty on seven counts\" while doc_0083, doc_0086, doc_0121 and doc_0293 all say \"is on trial for seven counts\" \u2014 the same paragraph carries the verdict in one document and the pending charge in four. All five blocks are assigned to one note so the temporal boundary survives. Two other blocks that read like chrome were assigned for the same reason: doc_0351[4], a \"note about discounted Intel-based Macs\" that inverts its own buying-guide framing (\"we don't recommend buying any older Intel-based Macs\"), and doc_0205[35], an \"Update October 11th\" line that dates the roundup.\n\nBELOW-90% DOCUMENTS \u2014 none, but four land under 96% and all four for the same honest reason: promotional furniture. doc_0461 (94.3%) is a Hot Pod newsletter whose dropped blocks are the \"Sign up here\" masthead and two item deks. doc_0521 (94.5%) and doc_0235 (94.9%) drop \"Verge Deals on X / Join more than 51,000 followers\", the email-signup boilerplate, and pure section headers/photo credits. doc_0069 (95.1%) drops the same X-follow block and a \"Some more deals to kickstart your weekend\" teaser. doc_0539 (96.3%) drops one-to-three-word conversational turns (\"Yeah.\", \"That's right.\", \"Thank you, I think\") and the Decoder podcast promo \u2014 179 words spread over 20 blocks, none carrying content.\n\nDEALS-ROUNDUP DECOMPOSITION. Five documents (doc_0069, doc_0205, doc_0235, doc_0351, doc_0521) are the failure case the brief warns about: one document, many unrelated items. I split each at the document's own category boundaries rather than merging \u2014 Prime Day gaming became six notes (video games / consoles+controllers / laptops+headsets / storage / tabletop / overview), never one. doc_0403 got the finest split, one note per retailer (15), because \"What is Target's price-match policy?\" must return the Target note and not a 2,100-word omnibus. doc_0461, a three-item newsletter, is three item notes plus one overview, never one note.\n\ndoc_0136 CONSTRAINT. This 7,088-word feature segments into only 18 blocks, eight of them 500\u20131,285 words. Since blocks are indivisible, the eight-note decomposition follows the article's own narrative sections; one note (rate_my_professors_harassment_of_janani_umamaheswar, 1,285 words) is a single block and sits below but near the density cap with no legal split point available."
